// rust/examples/error_conditioning_c3.rs  —  cargo run --example error_conditioning_c3
// One-pass vs two-pass variance, and the shift check.
// Note: .sum() adds left to right in plain doubles. R's sum() uses extended
// precision and numpy's uses pairwise summation, so *wrong* one-pass results
// can differ between R, Python and Rust; the two-pass results agree.

fn mean(x: &[f64]) -&gt; f64 {
    x.iter().sum::&lt;f64&gt;() / x.len() as f64
}

fn one_pass(x: &[f64]) -&gt; f64 {
    let sq: Vec&lt;f64&gt; = x.iter().map(|v| v * v).collect();
    mean(&sq) - mean(x).powi(2)
}

fn two_pass(x: &[f64]) -&gt; f64 {
    let m = mean(x);
    let dev2: Vec&lt;f64&gt; = x.iter().map(|v| (v - m).powi(2)).collect();
    mean(&dev2)
}

fn main() {
    // (a)
    for m in [1e4, 1e6, 1e8, 1e9] {
        // values m-3, ..., m+3, 100 of each; `i % 7` is the remainder
        let x: Vec&lt;f64&gt; = (0..700).map(|i| m + (i % 7) as f64 - 3.0).collect();
        println!("m = {:.0e}   one-pass = {}   two-pass = {}", m, one_pass(&x), two_pass(&x));
    }

    // (b)
    let x: Vec&lt;f64&gt; = [0.1, 0.2, 0.3].iter().map(|v| 7e8 + v).collect();
    println!("{}", one_pass(&x)); // one-pass
    println!("{}", two_pass(&x)); // two-pass

    // (c) shift check (Rust counts from 0, so x[0] is R's x[1])
    let x0: Vec&lt;f64&gt; = x.iter().map(|v| v - x[0]).collect();
    println!("{}", one_pass(&x0));
}

// Output:
// m = 1e4   one-pass = 4   two-pass = 4
// m = 1e6   one-pass = 4   two-pass = 4
// m = 1e8   one-pass = 2   two-pass = 4
// m = 1e9   one-pass = 0   two-pass = 4
// -64
// 0.0066666618982983055
// 0.0066666618982967295




One-pass loses about \(2\log_{10}(m/s)\) digits (\(s = 2\) here): at \(m = 10^8\) the answer is off by a factor 2, at \(10^9\) it is 0. Two-pass is right throughout.
A negative variance, \(-64\), which is impossible. The terms \(\overline{x^2}\) and \(\bar x^2\) are near \(4.9\times10^{17}\), where doubles are spaced 64 apart, so the one-pass answer can only be a multiple of 64. (The two-pass answer \(0.006666662\) instead of \(0.0066666\overline{6}\) is not the algorithm’s fault: \(7\times10^8 + 0.1\) cannot be stored exactly, so the stored data themselves are off by up to \(6\times10^{-8}\), which changes the variance in the 7th digit.)
After the shift the data are \(0, 0.1, 0.2\): no large common part is left, so there is nothing big to cancel. Variance does not change under a shift, so a disagreement between the shifted and unshifted results is a red flag. (Careful: with other data, R, numpy and Rust can give different wrong one-pass answers, because R’s sum accumulates in extended precision, numpy’s sums pairwise and Rust’s plain .sum() adds naively left to right. The correct two-pass answer agrees.)










Solution C4






RPythonRust


