Recap: elimination as LU
Row 2 minus m = a_{21}/a_{11} times row 1 (the pivot is a_{11}):
\begin{pmatrix}1 & 0.95\\ 0.95 & 1\end{pmatrix}
\;\xrightarrow{\;m = 0.95\;}\;
\begin{pmatrix}1 & 0.95\\ 0 & 1 - 0.95^2\end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix}1 & 0.95\\ 0 & 0.0975\end{pmatrix} = U,
\qquad L = \begin{pmatrix}1 & 0\\ 0.95 & 1\end{pmatrix}

A = LU. Solve L\mathbf y = \mathbf b (forward), then U\mathbf x = \mathbf y (back).
Each step divides by a pivot u_{kk}. Nothing so far checks its size.
Cost \tfrac23 n^3 for the factorisation, 2n^2 per right-hand side (from Gaussian elimination as LU).


One-slide recap only; the previous unit did this properly. The point to carry forward: the multipliers are entries divided by the pivot, and back substitution divides by the pivots again. If a pivot is zero the method stops; if it is tiny, we will see that it silently returns garbage. Note that the second pivot 0.0975 is already small: the near-singularity of A shows up in U.

What went wrong
With \delta = 10^{-20}: multiplier m = 1/\delta = 10^{20}.
\begin{pmatrix}\delta & 1 & \mid & 1\\ 1 & 1 & \mid & 2\end{pmatrix}
\;\to\;
\begin{pmatrix}\delta & 1 & \mid & 1\\ 0 & 1 - 10^{20} & \mid & 2 - 10^{20}\end{pmatrix}
\;\approx\;
\begin{pmatrix}\delta & 1 & \mid & 1\\ 0 & -10^{20} & \mid & -10^{20}\end{pmatrix}

1 - 10^{20} rounds to -10^{20}: the 1 and the 2 of row 2 are lost (they are below the gap between doubles near 10^{20}).
x_2 = 1 (fine), then x_1 = (1 - x_2)/\delta = 0/\delta = 0: catastrophic cancellation, divided by a tiny number.
The computed L and U belong to a different matrix: LU has 0 where A has 1 in position (2,2).


Do this on the board. The huge multiplier copies row 1, scaled by 1e20, into row 2 and swamps what row 2 contained. The second equation is effectively replaced by a multiple of the first, so the information that x1 + x2 = 2 is gone. Back substitution then cancels 1 - 1 = 0. Check LU: L = [1 0; 1e20 1], U = [1e-20 1; 0 -1e20], LU = [1e-20 1; 1 0]. The (2,2) entry 1 has vanished: the backward error is 100 %, which is the precise meaning of “unstable”.

Partial pivoting
Before eliminating column k, swap rows so the entry of largest absolute value in that column (on or below the diagonal) becomes the pivot.
\begin{pmatrix}\delta & 1 & \mid & 1\\ 1 & 1 & \mid & 2\end{pmatrix}
\;\xrightarrow{\text{swap}}\;
\begin{pmatrix}1 & 1 & \mid & 2\\ \delta & 1 & \mid & 1\end{pmatrix}
\;\xrightarrow{m = \delta}\;
\begin{pmatrix}1 & 1 & \mid & 2\\ 0 & 1 - \delta & \mid & 1 - 2\delta\end{pmatrix}

Now x_2 = (1-2\delta)/(1-\delta) \approx 1, x_1 = 2 - x_2 \approx 1. Correct.
Every multiplier satisfies |m| \le 1, so no row is scaled up and swamps another.
Swapping rows = reordering the equations (detectors). The solution does not change.


The rule is simple and cheap: one search down a column per step, O(n^2) in total, nothing next to the O(n^3) elimination. Bounded multipliers mean the entries of U cannot grow much in practice, which is what keeps the rounding errors small. “Partial” because only rows are swapped; complete pivoting also swaps columns and is rarely worth it.

Partial pivoting in code
solve_pivot &lt;- function(A, b) {
  n &lt;- nrow(A)
  for (k in 1:(n - 1)) {
    p &lt;- which.max(abs(A[k:n, k])) + k - 1   # largest entry in column k
    A[c(k, p), ] &lt;- A[c(p, k), ];  b[c(k, p)] &lt;- b[c(p, k)]   # swap rows
    for (i in (k + 1):n) {
      m &lt;- A[i, k] / A[k, k]                 # now |m| &lt;= 1
      A[i, ] &lt;- A[i, ] - m * A[k, ];  b[i] &lt;- b[i] - m * b[k]
    }
  }
  backsolve(A, b)
}
solve_pivot(A1, c(1, 2))
# [1] 1 1

Three extra lines. solve() calls LAPACK (dgesv), which does exactly this, in optimised code.


which.max returns the position within the sub-column k:n, so we add k - 1 to get the row number. The swap uses R’s vector indexing: A[c(k, p), ] &lt;- A[c(p, k), ] exchanges two rows in one line. Students write this themselves in the exercise (C2) and compare with solve(). Python: scipy.linalg.lu_factor / lu_solve and numpy.linalg.solve use the same LAPACK routine.

Fixed-point iteration in R
fixed_point &lt;- function(g, x0, tol = 1e-10, maxit = 1000) {
  x &lt;- x0
  for (k in 1:maxit) {
    x_new &lt;- g(x)
    if (abs(x_new - x) &lt; tol) return(c(root = x_new, iter = k))
    x &lt;- x_new
  }
  warning("no convergence after ", maxit, " steps"); c(root = x, iter = maxit)
}
print(fixed_point(g, 30), digits = 12)       # the harvested stock
#          root          iter
# 74.9524424992 82.0000000000

Stops when the step |x_{k+1} - x_k| is small. That is not the error: here the true error is 2.0\times10^{-10}, twice tol.
82 steps for 10 digits: |g'(N^*)| = 0.71 here, so each step gains only -\log_{10} 0.71 = 0.15 digits.


The stopping rule is the step size, because the root is unknown. When convergence is linear with rate L, the remaining error is about step × L/(1 - L): here 0.71/0.29 = 2.4 times the last step. For L = 0.99 the error is 99 times the step, so a small step can be a long way from the answer. We come back to estimating L from the iterates on the “Checking” slide. maxit plus a warning is again essential: if g has no attracting fixed point, the loop would otherwise run forever.

More equations than unknowns
Each standard gives one equation x_1 + x_2 \log_{10} N_i = \text{Cq}_i. Stacked:

\underbrace{\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1\\ 1 & 1\\ 1 & 1\\ 1 & 2\\ \vdots & \vdots\\ 1 & 7 \end{pmatrix}}_{A,\ 21\times 2}
\begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \end{pmatrix}
\approx
\underbrace{\begin{pmatrix} 34.52\\ 34.69\\ 34.48\\ 31.62\\ \vdots\\ 14.73 \end{pmatrix}}_{\mathbf b,\ 21\times 1}


A is the design matrix (in R: model.matrix). One row per measurement, one column per parameter.
m = 21 rows, n = 2 columns: overdetermined. The first three rows already disagree (34.52, 34.69, 34.48 for the same x), so there is no exact solution.
We need a rule that picks the “best” \mathbf x.


Write the matrix with the students. The column of ones is the intercept; it is what lm adds silently. Every linear model, including polynomial fits, multiple regression and ANOVA, has this form: the model is linear in the parameters, even if not in the predictor. More rows than columns is the normal case in data analysis: we measure more than we estimate, so that noise averages out.

Gram–Schmidt in R: classical vs modified
gs &lt;- function(A, modified = FALSE) {
  n &lt;- ncol(A); Q &lt;- A; R &lt;- matrix(0, n, n)
  for (j in 1:n) {
    v &lt;- A[, j]
    for (i in seq_len(j - 1)) {
      R[i, j] &lt;- sum(Q[, i] * (if (modified) v else A[, j]))
      v &lt;- v - R[i, j] * Q[, i]
    }
    R[j, j] &lt;- sqrt(sum(v^2)); Q[, j] &lt;- v / R[j, j]
  }
  list(Q = Q, R = R)
}

Classical (CGS): project the original column \mathbf a_j on each \mathbf q_i.
Modified (MGS): project the current \mathbf v, already cleaned of \mathbf q_1, \dots, \mathbf q_{i-1}. Same in exact arithmetic; one word differs in the code.


Write it yourself first, then use the library: this is the hand-written version. In exact arithmetic the two give identical Q and R, because q_i is orthogonal to the parts already removed. With rounding, the q_i are only nearly orthogonal, and CGS lets those errors add up. MGS removes each component from the current vector, so it also removes the rounding errors left by earlier steps. Python and Rust versions are in the exercise.

Background
A standard curve relates a known amount (copies of a qPCR standard, µg/mL of BSA) to a reading (Cq, absorbance). With \(m\) standards and a model with \(n\) parameters, \(m &gt; n\), we get an overdetermined system
\[A\mathbf x \approx \mathbf b, \qquad A: m\times n \text{ (design matrix)}, \quad \mathbf b: \text{the readings}.\]
Facts used below:

Residual and least squares. \(\mathbf r = \mathbf b - A\mathbf x\). Least squares minimises \(\|\mathbf r\|^2 = \sum_i r_i^2\). At the minimum the residual is perpendicular to every column of \(A\): \(A^T\mathbf r = \mathbf 0\).
Normal equations. \(A^TA\,\mathbf x = A^T\mathbf b\), an \(n\times n\) symmetric positive definite system. For a straight line \(b \approx x_1 + x_2 t\): \[A^TA = \begin{pmatrix} m & \sum t_i \\ \sum t_i & \sum t_i^2\end{pmatrix}, \qquad A^T\mathbf b = \begin{pmatrix}\sum b_i \\ \sum t_i b_i\end{pmatrix}.\]
Condition number. \(\kappa(A)\) = largest stretch / smallest stretch of \(A\) over unit vectors (kappa(A, exact = TRUE), np.linalg.cond(A)). A good method loses about \(\log_{10}\kappa(A)\) digits. Since \(\|A\mathbf v\|^2 = \mathbf v^TA^TA\mathbf v\), \(\kappa(A^TA) = \kappa(A)^2\): the normal equations lose about twice as many.
QR. \(A = QR\) with \(Q^TQ = I\) (\(m\times n\), orthonormal columns) and \(R\) upper triangular (\(n\times n\)). Least squares becomes \(R\mathbf x = Q^T\mathbf b\) (back substitution), and \(\kappa(R) = \kappa(A)\).
Gram–Schmidt. For \(j = 1, \dots, n\): \(\mathbf v = \mathbf a_j\); for each earlier \(i\): \(r_{ij} = \mathbf q_i^T\mathbf a_j\) (classical) or \(\mathbf q_i^T\mathbf v\) (modified), \(\mathbf v \leftarrow \mathbf v - r_{ij}\mathbf q_i\); then \(r_{jj} = \|\mathbf v\|\), \(\mathbf q_j = \mathbf v / r_{jj}\).
Householder reflection. \(H = I - 2\mathbf v\mathbf v^T/(\mathbf v^T\mathbf v)\) with \(\mathbf v = \mathbf a + \text{sign}(a_1)\|\mathbf a\|\mathbf e_1\) maps \(\mathbf a\) to \(-\text{sign}(a_1)\|\mathbf a\|\mathbf e_1\). Useful form: \(H\mathbf a = \mathbf a - 2\dfrac{\mathbf v^T\mathbf a}{\mathbf v^T\mathbf v}\mathbf v\).
qPCR. \(\text{Cq} = x_1 + x_2\log_{10}N\); efficiency \(E = 10^{-1/x_2} - 1\) (slope \(-3.32\) gives 100 %).

The two data sets used throughout and the basic calls in each language: qPCR standards (\(10^1\) to \(10^7\) copies, triplicates) and BCA protein standards (BSA 0–2000 µg/mL, duplicates, absorbance at 562 nm).

