Brent’s method needs 9 or 10 iterations where bisection needs about 46 for the same accuracy (from a bracket of width 50 down to \(10^{-12}\)). uniroot’s default tolerance (\(\varepsilon^{1/4} \approx 1.2\times10^{-4}\)) gives errors of \(10^{-7}\) to \(10^{-6}\); brentq’s defaults (xtol = 2e-12, rtol \(\approx 9\times10^{-16}\)) are already tight. In Rust, BrentRoot::new(a, b, tol) takes the tolerance explicitly; \(10^{-4}\) gives 8 iterations and an error of \(8\times10^{-7}\) for the upper root. All three refuse to start without a sign change, like your bisect.

