B1. (a) \(A^TA = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & 4 \\ 0 & 5\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 0 \\ 4 & 5\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 25 & 20 \\ 20 & 25\end{pmatrix}\). \(\lambda^2 - 50\lambda + 225 = 0\), so \(\lambda = 45\) and \(5\). \(\sigma_1 = \sqrt{45} = 6.708\), \(\sigma_2 = \sqrt5 = 2.236\). (b) \(\lambda = 45\): \(-20v_{1} + 20v_{2} = 0\), so \(v_1 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}(1, 1)\). \(\lambda = 5\): \(20v_1 + 20v_2 = 0\), so \(v_2 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}(1, -1)\). Then \(Av_1 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}(3, 9)\), and dividing by \(\sqrt{45}\): \(u_1 = (3, 9)/\sqrt{90} = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{10}}(1, 3) = (0.316, 0.949)\). \(Av_2 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}(3, -1)\), divided by \(\sqrt5\): \(u_2 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{10}}(3, -1) = (0.949, -0.316)\). \(u_1^Tu_2 = (3 - 3)/10 = 0\). (c) \(\sigma_1u_1v_1^T = \tfrac{\sqrt{45}}{\sqrt{20}}\begin{pmatrix}1 & 1\\ 3 & 3\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}1.5 & 1.5\\ 4.5 & 4.5\end{pmatrix}\) and \(\sigma_2u_2v_2^T = \tfrac{\sqrt5}{\sqrt{20}}\begin{pmatrix}3 & -3\\ -1 & 1\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}1.5 & -1.5\\ -0.5 & 0.5\end{pmatrix}\). The sum is \(\begin{pmatrix}3 & 0\\ 4 & 5\end{pmatrix}\). (d) \(\|A\|_2 = 6.708\), \(\kappa = \sqrt{45/5} = 3\). \(\sigma_1\sigma_2 = \sqrt{225} = 15 = |3\cdot5 - 0\cdot4|\). (e) Half-axes \(\sigma_1u_1 = (2.12, 6.36)\) (length 6.71) and \(\sigma_2u_2 = (2.12, -0.71)\) (length 2.24). The unit vector stretched most is \(v_1 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}(1,1)\) (or \(-v_1\)); it lands on the long half-axis. This is the right-hand panel of the rotate–stretch–rotate figure.
B2. (a) Rank 1: the columns are equal. \(A^TA = \begin{pmatrix}9 & 9\\ 9 & 9\end{pmatrix}\), eigenvalues 18 and 0, so \(\sigma_1 = \sqrt{18} = 3\sqrt2 = 4.243\), \(\sigma_2 = 0\). (b) \(v_1 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}(1, 1)\), \(v_2 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}(1, -1)\). \(u_1 = Av_1/\sigma_1 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}(2, 4, 4)/(3\sqrt2) = \tfrac13(1, 2, 2)\). \(v_2\) is “more of type 1, less of type 2”; \(Av_2 = 0\), so \(A\) cannot see it at all. (c) \(b = 3\cdot(1, 2, 2)\) is exactly in the range of \(A\): every \(x\) with \(x_1 + x_2 = 3\) fits with residual 0. These solutions form a line, \(x = (1.5, 1.5) + t\,v_2\). (d) \(A^+ = \frac{1}{3\sqrt2}\cdot\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\end{pmatrix}\tfrac13(1, 2, 2) = \tfrac{1}{18}\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 2\\ 1 & 2 & 2\end{pmatrix}\). \(A^+b = \tfrac{1}{18}(3 + 12 + 12)(1, 1) = (1.5, 1.5)\). On the line \(x_1 + x_2 = 3\), \(\|x\|^2 = x_1^2 + (3-x_1)^2\) is smallest at \(x_1 = 1.5\): yes, the minimum-norm solution, an equal split. (e) \(A^+b = \tfrac{1}{18}(3 + 12 + 14)(1, 1) = (1.611, 1.611)\). The noise changes the total, never the split: the split is set by the convention, not by the data.
B3. (a) \(\lambda^2 - 2.02\lambda + 0.02 = 0\): \(\lambda = \tfrac12(2.02 \pm \sqrt{4.0804 - 0.08}) = \tfrac12(2.02 \pm 2.0001)\), so \(\sigma_1 = 2.01\) and \(\sigma_2 = 0.00995\). \(\kappa = 2.01/0.00995 = 202\). (b) \(A^{-1} = \frac{1}{0.02}\begin{pmatrix}1.02 & -1\\ -1 & 1\end{pmatrix}\). \(x = 50\,(2.04 - 2.02,\; -2 + 2.02) = (1, 1)\). (c) \(x = 50\,(2.04 - 2.03,\; -2 + 2.03) = (0.5, 1.5)\). Relative change in \(b\): \(0.01/\|(2, 2.02)\| = 0.01/2.843 = 0.35\%\). In \(x\): \(\|(-0.5, 0.5)\|/\|(1,1)\| = 0.707/1.414 = 50\%\). Amplification \(50/0.35 = 141 &lt; \kappa = 202\). (d) \((A - 0.00995I)v = 0\): \(0.99005\,v_1 + v_2 = 0\), so \(v \propto (1, -0.990)\), nearly \(\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}(1, -1)\). The change in (c), \((-0.5, 0.5)\), is along this direction: a 0.5 % error in one gene moved half a unit from cell type 1 to cell type 2 and left the total unchanged.
B4. (a) \(u_1^Tb \approx (2 + 2.03)/\sqrt2 = 2.850\); divided by \(\sigma_1\): \(1.418\); times \(v_1\): \(x_1 \approx (1.002, 1.002)\). (The exact \(u_1, v_1\) are slightly tilted; C-code gives \((0.997, 1.007)\).) The noise is gone: the answer is close to \((1, 1)\), as for the clean \(b\). (b) \(\lambda = 0.1\): \(f_1 = 4.0401/(4.0401 + 0.01) = 0.9975\), \(f_2 = 0.000099/(0.000099 + 0.01) = 0.0098\). \(\lambda = 1\): \(f_1 = 4.0401/5.0401 = 0.80\), \(f_2 = 0.000099/1.000099 \approx 0.0001\). (c) Ridge multiplies the \(v_2\)-component by \(f_2 \approx 0.0098\), so the move shrinks from \((-0.5, 0.5)\) to about \((-0.005, 0.005)\). (d) \(x = (1.2, 0.8)\) gives \(b = (2, 2.016)\); \(u_1^Tb/\sigma_1 \approx 4.016/(\sqrt2 \cdot 2.01)\), times \(v_1\): about \((1.0, 1.0)\). TSVD reports an equal split whatever the truth: the real difference is along \(v_2\) and is thrown away together with the noise. That is the bias of regularisation. With \(\lambda = 1\) ridge also shrinks the \(v_1\) component by \(f_1 = 0.8\), so even the total is too small: too much regularisation.
B5. (a) \(A^TA = \begin{pmatrix}3 & 3.1\\ 3.1 & 3.21\end{pmatrix}\), \(A^Tb = (2.9, 2.99)\), \(\det = 9.63 - 9.61 = 0.02\). \(x = \frac{1}{0.02}(3.21\cdot2.9 - 3.1\cdot2.99,\; -3.1\cdot2.9 + 3\cdot2.99) = 50\,(0.04, -0.02) = (2, -1)\). It fits exactly (\(Ax = (1, 1, 0.9)\)), but \(x_2 &lt; 0\) is not a proportion. (b) \(x_1 = a_1^Tb/a_1^Ta_1 = 2.9/3 = 0.9667\), \(x = (0.9667, 0)\). (c) \(b - Ax = (0.0333, 0.0333, -0.0667)\); \(w_2 = a_2^T(b - Ax) = 0.0333 + 0.0333 - 1.1\cdot0.0667 = -0.0067 &lt; 0\). Increasing \(x_2\) would increase the residual, so \((0.9667, 0)\) is the NNLS solution. (\(w_1 = 0\), as it must be for a free variable.) (d) A 50/50 mix with total 0.9667 would be \((0.483, 0.483)\). Least squares said \((2, -1)\) (total 1), NNLS says \((0.967, 0)\) (total 0.967). Both get the total about right; neither gets the split. Gene 3 is the only gene that distinguishes the two types, and its value alone decides the answer.
B6. (a) Gene 1: \((142.074 - 139.232)/\sqrt2 = 2.010\). Gene 2: \((145.465 - 148.374)/\sqrt2 = -2.057\). All other genes: 0. Length \(\sqrt{2.010^2 + 2.057^2} = 2.876 \approx \sigma_5\). So \(u_5 \approx (0.699, -0.715, 0, \dots, 0)\): the data direction of the CD4 − CD8 difference is “gene 1 minus gene 2”. (b) Noise standard deviations \(0.05\cdot65 = 3.25\) and \(0.05\cdot67 = 3.35\). \(u_5^T\delta b = 0.699\,\delta b_1 - 0.715\,\delta b_2\) has standard deviation \(\sqrt{0.699^2\cdot3.25^2 + 0.715^2\cdot3.35^2} = \sqrt{5.16 + 5.74} = 3.3\). (c) Error along \(v_5\): \(3.3/2.875 = 1.15\). CD4 and CD8 each move by \(1.15/\sqrt2 = 0.81\) (in opposite directions); CD4 + CD8 does not move. So least-squares CD4 and CD8 estimates have a standard deviation of about 0.8, for proportions of 0.30 and 0.15. C7 confirms this (0.86 in R). (d) \(40 \times 2.22\times10^{-16} \times 642.9 = 5.7\times10^{-12}\); four singular values are above it, so the numerical rank is 4. \(\sigma_5\) is not 0 because \(S\) was built with rounding errors (the sin values and products) and the SVD itself rounds; \(6.6\times10^{-14}\) is about \(\varepsilon\,\sigma_1\) times a small factor, i.e. zero to working precision.









