// rust/examples/newton_c6.rs  —  cargo run --example newton_c6
// C6: count function evaluations: Brent (argmin's BrentRoot), Newton, secant.
use argmin::core::{CostFunction, Error, Executor};
use argmin::solver::brent::BrentRoot;
use std::cell::Cell;
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, Ordering};

// global evaluation counter for the argmin run (argmin owns the function object)
static BRENT_EVALS: AtomicU64 = AtomicU64::new(0);

fn f(c: f64) -&gt; f64 {
    0.3 * c / (0.1 + c) + 0.7 * c.powi(2) / (25.0 + c.powi(2)) - 0.5
}
fn df(c: f64) -&gt; f64 {
    0.03 / (0.1 + c).powi(2) + 35.0 * c / (25.0 + c.powi(2)).powi(2)
}

fn newton(f: impl Fn(f64) -&gt; f64, df: impl Fn(f64) -&gt; f64, x0: f64, tol: f64, maxit: usize) -&gt; f64 {
    let mut x = x0;
    for _ in 0..maxit {
        let step = f(x) / df(x);
        x -= step;
        if step.abs() &lt;= tol * x.abs() {
            break;
        }
    }
    x
}

fn secant(f: impl Fn(f64) -&gt; f64, mut x0: f64, mut x1: f64, tol: f64, maxit: usize) -&gt; f64 {
    let (mut f0, mut f1) = (f(x0), f(x1));
    for _ in 0..maxit {
        if f1 == f0 {
            break;
        }
        let step = f1 * (x1 - x0) / (f1 - f0);
        x0 = x1;
        f0 = f1;
        x1 -= step;
        f1 = f(x1);
        if step.abs() &lt;= tol * x1.abs() {
            break;
        }
    }
    x1
}

struct TwoSite;
impl CostFunction for TwoSite {
    type Param = f64;
    type Output = f64;
    fn cost(&self, c: &f64) -&gt; Result&lt;f64, Error&gt; {
        BRENT_EVALS.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
        Ok(f(*c))
    }
}

fn main() {
    let c50 = 3.261305265055212;
    let report = |name: &str, evals: u64, root: f64| {
        println!("{:&lt;22} evals {:3}   error {:.1e}", name, evals, (root - c50).abs());
    };
    for tol in [1e-4, 1e-12] {
        let res = Executor::new(TwoSite, BrentRoot::new(0.01, 100.0, tol))
            .configure(|s| s.max_iters(100))
            .run()
            .unwrap();
        let evals = BRENT_EVALS.swap(0, Ordering::Relaxed);
        report(&format!("BrentRoot, tol = {:.0e}", tol), evals, res.state.best_param.unwrap());
    }
    // a Cell is a counter that closures may change without `mut`
    let count = Cell::new(0u64);
    let fc = |c: f64| { count.set(count.get() + 1); f(c) };
    let dfc = |c: f64| { count.set(count.get() + 1); df(c) };
    let r = newton(fc, dfc, 1.0, 1e-12, 50);
    report("Newton from 1", count.replace(0), r);
    let r = secant(fc, 1.0, 2.0, 1e-12, 50);
    report("secant from 1, 2", count.replace(0), r);
}

// Output:
// BrentRoot, tol = 1e-4  evals  11   error 1.4e-7
// BrentRoot, tol = 1e-12 evals  12   error 4.4e-16
// Newton from 1          evals  10   error 0.0e0
// secant from 1, 2       evals   8   error 4.4e-16



All reach \(c_{50}\) with 7–13 evaluations from a bracket 10 000 times wider than the answer; bisection would need about 58. With the default tol, uniroot stops at an error of \(1.4\times10^{-7}\) (brentq’s default xtol is \(2\times10^{-12}\), much tighter). The secant method is cheapest (8 evaluations, 7 in scipy.optimize.newton, which picks its own second point), then Newton (10: five steps of \(f\) and \(f'\)). Brent’s method costs a few evaluations more but is the only one that cannot fail: it never leaves the bracket. That is why the libraries use it by default, and why our hand-written methods need a good start.

Background
Facts used below:

SPD. \(A\) is symmetric positive definite if \(A = A^T\) and \(\mathbf{x}^TA\mathbf{x} &gt; 0\) for every \(\mathbf{x} \ne \mathbf{0}\); equivalently, all eigenvalues are positive. Covariance and kinship matrices are SPD, unless they are estimated from too few observations (then only semidefinite).
Cholesky. Every SPD matrix is \(A = LL^T\) with \(L\) lower triangular and positive diagonal. Column by column: \[\ell_{jj} = \sqrt{a_{jj} - \textstyle\sum_{k&lt;j}\ell_{jk}^2}, \qquad \ell_{ij} = \Big(a_{ij} - \textstyle\sum_{k&lt;j}\ell_{ik}\ell_{jk}\Big)\Big/\ell_{jj}\quad (i&gt;j).\] Cost \(\tfrac13 n^3\), no pivoting. A square root of a number \(\le 0\) means \(A\) is not SPD.
Using \(L\). Solve \(A\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{b}\) by \(L\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{b}\) (forward) then \(L^T\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{y}\) (back). \(\log\det A = 2\sum_i \log\ell_{ii}\). If \(\mathbf{z}\) has independent \(N(0,1)\) entries, \(L\mathbf{z}\) has covariance \(A\).
Thomas algorithm for a tridiagonal system with sub-diagonal \(a_i\), diagonal \(b_i\), super-diagonal \(c_i\), right-hand side \(d_i\): forward sweep \(w = a_i/b_{i-1}\), \(b_i \leftarrow b_i - w c_{i-1}\), \(d_i \leftarrow d_i - w d_{i-1}\) for \(i = 2,\dots,n\); back substitution \(x_n = d_n/b_n\), \(x_i = (d_i - c_i x_{i+1})/b_i\). About \(8n\) operations. No pivoting: safe if \(|b_i| \ge |a_i| + |c_i|\) in every row (strictly in at least one) or if the matrix is SPD.
Morphogen in a row of cells (lecture): \(-D\,c_{i-1} + (2D+k)\,c_i - D\,c_{i+1} = s_i\), with \(D + k\) on the diagonal of the two end cells. We use \(D = 1\), \(k = 0.01\) per minute, production \(s_1 = 1\) in cell 1.
CSC stores the non-zeros column by column: x (values), i (row index, from 0), and p (column pointers, length = columns + 1, from 0): column \(j\) (from 0) owns entries p[j] to p[j+1] - 1. About 12 bytes per non-zero.
Fill-in: zeros of \(A\) that become non-zero in \(L\) (or \(U\)). It depends on the order of the unknowns. The inverse of a sparse matrix is usually completely dense.
Jacobi: \(\mathbf{x} \leftarrow \mathbf{x} + D^{-1}(\mathbf{b} - A\mathbf{x})\), \(D\) = diagonal of \(A\). Converges for diagonally dominant \(A\). Conjugate gradient (CG) needs SPD \(A\). Both need only products \(A\mathbf{x}\).

The basic tools in each language. R’s chol returns the upper factor \(R = L^T\); Python’s np.linalg.cholesky and nalgebra’s Cholesky return the lower factor \(L\).

