Solution





B1. (a) \(\ell_{11} = \sqrt4 = 2\), \(\ell_{21} = 2/2 = 1\), \(\ell_{31} = -2/2 = -1\). \(\ell_{22} = \sqrt{10 - 1} = 3\), \(\ell_{32} = (2 - (-1)(1))/3 = 1\). \(\ell_{33} = \sqrt{6 - 1 - 1} = 2\). \[L = \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 0 & 0 \\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\ -1 & 1 & 2 \end{pmatrix}.\] (b) \(L\mathbf{y} = (4, 14, 6)\): \(y_1 = 4/2 = 2\), \(y_2 = (14 - 2)/3 = 4\), \(y_3 = (6 + 2 - 4)/2 = 2\). \(L^T\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{y}\): \(x_3 = 2/2 = 1\), \(x_2 = (4 - 1\cdot 1)/3 = 1\), \(x_1 = (2 - 1 + 1)/2 = 1\). So \(\mathbf{x} = (1, 1, 1)\). (c) \(\det\Sigma = (2\cdot3\cdot2)^2 = 144\), \(\log\det\Sigma = 2(\log 2 + \log 3 + \log 2) = 2\log 12 = 4.970\). Directly: \(4(60 - 4) - 2(12 + 4) + (-2)(4 + 20) = 224 - 32 - 48 = 144\). (d) \(\ell_{11} = 1\), \(\ell_{21} = 0.9\), \(\ell_{31} = 0.9\), \(\ell_{22} = \sqrt{1 - 0.81} = 0.4359\), \(\ell_{32} = (-0.9 - 0.81)/0.4359 = -3.923\), and \(\ell_{33}^2 = 1 - 0.81 - 15.39 = -15.2 &lt; 0\): no real square root, the matrix is not positive definite. In words: if gene 1 rises with gene 2 and with gene 3 (both \(r = 0.9\)), then genes 2 and 3 must also rise together; they cannot be strongly anti-correlated. Correlations estimated pairwise on different samples need not fit together.
B2. (a) \(a = (\cdot, -1, -1, -1)\), \(b = (2, 3, 3, 2)\), \(c = (-1, -1, -1, \cdot)\), \(d = (1, 0, 0, 0)\). (b) \(i = 2\): \(w = -1/2\), \(b_2 = 3 - \tfrac12 = \tfrac52\), \(d_2 = 0 + \tfrac12 = \tfrac12\). \(i = 3\): \(w = -1/\tfrac52 = -\tfrac25\), \(b_3 = 3 - \tfrac25 = \tfrac{13}5\), \(d_3 = \tfrac25\cdot\tfrac12 = \tfrac15\). \(i = 4\): \(w = -\tfrac5{13}\), \(b_4 = 2 - \tfrac5{13} = \tfrac{21}{13}\), \(d_4 = \tfrac5{13}\cdot\tfrac15 = \tfrac1{13}\). (c) \(c_4 = \tfrac1{13}/\tfrac{21}{13} = \tfrac1{21}\). \(c_3 = (\tfrac15 + \tfrac1{21})/\tfrac{13}5 = \tfrac2{21}\). \(c_2 = (\tfrac12 + \tfrac2{21})/\tfrac52 = \tfrac5{21}\). \(c_1 = (1 + \tfrac5{21})/2 = \tfrac{13}{21}\). (d) \(k\sum c_i = (13 + 5 + 2 + 1)/21 = 1\). Yes: everything produced is degraded. (e) Yes: \(2 \ge 1\), \(3 \ge 2\), \(3 \ge 2\), \(2 \ge 1\), strictly in every row. The pivots \(2, \tfrac52, \tfrac{13}5, \tfrac{21}{13}\) are all positive (and all \(\ge 1\)): diagonal dominance keeps them away from zero.
B3. (a) \(w = 1/1 = 1\), \(b_2 = 1 - 1\cdot1 = 0\). At \(i = 3\), \(w = 1/0\): division by zero, and every result becomes NaN. (b) Not diagonally dominant (\(1 &lt; 1 + 1\) in row 2). \(\det = 1(1 - 1) - 1(1 - 0) + 0 = -1 \ne 0\): not singular, it has a unique solution, \((-1, 2, 1)\) for \(d = (1, 2, 3)\). (c) LU with partial pivoting: it swaps rows so that the pivot is the largest available entry in the column, so a zero (or tiny) pivot never gets used while a better one is available. That is what solve and np.linalg.solve do.
B4. (a) x = (4, 1, 2, 6, 3, 1), i = (1, 0, 2, 1, 0, 2), p = (0, 1, 3, 3, 4, 6). (b) Cell 1: x[0] = 4. Cell 2: x[1] + x[2] = 3. Cell 3: p[2] = p[3], empty, 0. Cell 4: 6. Cell 5: \(3 + 1 = 4\). (c) Column 1 has one entry, 5 in row 2 (0-based); column 2 is empty; column 3 has 1 in row 0 and 2 in row 1: \[\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\ 5 & 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}.\] (d) Dense: \(2\times10^9 \times 8 = 16\) GB. CSC: \(0.07 \times 2\times10^9 = 1.4\times10^8\) non-zeros × 12 bytes = 1.68 GB, plus \(100\,001 \times 4\) bytes ≈ 0.4 MB of pointers. CSC is larger than dense when \(12f &gt; 8\), i.e. \(f &gt; 2/3\).
B5. (a) Row \(i\) (\(i = 2, 3, 4\)) minus a multiple of row 1 becomes non-zero in columns 2, 3, 4: every zero in the lower right 3 × 3 block fills in. \(L\) is full lower triangular (10 non-zeros, of which 3 are fill-in in \(L\)). (b) Hub last: \[\begin{pmatrix} \times & \cdot & \cdot & \times \\ \cdot & \times & \cdot & \times \\ \cdot & \cdot & \times & \times \\ \times & \times & \times & \times \end{pmatrix}.\] Eliminating column 1 only changes row 4, in column 4 (already non-zero). Same for columns 2 and 3. No fill-in; \(L\) has the diagonal plus the last row: \(2n - 1 = 7\) non-zeros. (c) No. Each step subtracts a multiple of row \(i-1\), which is non-zero only in columns \(i-1\) and \(i\), from row \(i\), which is already non-zero there. Fill-in is zero; the band is preserved. (d) Check: \(\begin{pmatrix} 2 & -1 & 0 \\ -1 & 2 & -1 \\ 0 & -1 & 2 \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}\), and similarly for the other columns, so the product is \(4I\). The inverse has no zeros, although \(A\) has 2.
B6. (a) Step 1: \(x_1 = 1/4 = 0.25\), \(x_2 = 2/3 = 0.667\). Step 2: \(x_1 = (1 - 0.667)/4 = 0.0833\), \(x_2 = (2 - 0.25)/3 = 0.583\). Exact: \((0.0909, 0.636)\). The error shrinks, from \((0.16, 0.03)\) to \((0.008, 0.05)\). (b) Step 1: \(x = (3, 3)\). Step 2: \(x_1 = 3 - 2\cdot3 = -3\), \(x_2 = -3\). Step 3: \(x = (9, 9)\). The error \(x_1 - 1\) goes \(2, -4, 8, \dots\): it doubles and changes sign at every step, so the iteration diverges. The first matrix is diagonally dominant (\(4 &gt; 1\), \(3 &gt; 1\)), the second is not (\(1 &lt; 2\)).
B7. (a) \(n = 10^4\): LU \(\tfrac23 10^{12}\) operations = 667 s ≈ 11 min; Cholesky 333 s; Thomas \(8\times10^4\) operations = 80 µs. (b) Dense: \(10^8 \times 8\) = 800 MB. Three diagonals: \(3\times10^4 \times 8\) = 240 kB. (c) \(n = 10^6\): LU \(6.7\times10^{17}\) operations = \(6.7\times10^8\) s ≈ 21 years; Cholesky 10 years; Thomas 8 ms. Dense memory 8 TB, three diagonals 24 MB.
