The arrays match B4(a). The tridiagonal matrix has 298 non-zeros, its inverse all 10 000: a source in any cell raises the level in every other cell, so no entry of \(A^{-1}\) is zero (the smallest, \(9\times10^{-4}\), is the effect of cell 1 on cell 100). For \(10^6\) cells the inverse would take 8 TB, the matrix itself 36 MB. Solve with solve(A, b), never with the inverse.
