// rust/examples/structured_sparse_c7.rs  —  cargo run --release --example structured_sparse_c7
// C7: fill-in for an arrow matrix (hub gene). sprs has no sparse Cholesky
// (the separate sprs-ldl crate has one), so we run nalgebra's dense Cholesky and
// count the non-zeros of L: the fill-in pattern is the same.
use nalgebra::DMatrix;

fn arrow(n: usize, hub: usize) -&gt; DMatrix&lt;f64&gt; {
    DMatrix::from_fn(n, n, |i, j| {
        if i == hub && j == hub { n as f64 }
        else if i == hub || j == hub { 1.0 }
        else if i == j { 2.0 } else { 0.0 }
    })
}

fn nnz(m: &DMatrix&lt;f64&gt;) -&gt; usize {
    m.iter().filter(|v| **v != 0.0).count()
}

fn main() {
    let n = 1000;
    println!("{}", nnz(&arrow(n, 0)));
    let l_first = arrow(n, 0).cholesky().unwrap().l();    // hub first
    let l_last = arrow(n, n - 1).cholesky().unwrap().l(); // hub last
    println!("{} {}", nnz(&l_first), nnz(&l_last));
}

// Output:
// 2998
// 500500 1999



Same matrix, same solution, but with the hub first the factor is completely full (\(n(n+1)/2 = 500\,500\) entries, 250 times more than \(A\)’s 2998), and with the hub last it has \(2n - 1 = 1999\), as B5 predicts. The ordering routines in Cholesky(perm = TRUE) and splu(permc_spec="COLAMD") find the good order by themselves (R’s perm is 0-based: the last unknown eliminated is unknown 0 + 1 = the hub). The general check: compare the non-zeros of the factor with those of \(A\).
